  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "What a Trait Is",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Contract",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Trait = named set of method signatures a type can implement.",
            "Rust's shared-behavior abstraction. Like an interface, not a base class.",
            "Traits are not types. `Summary` names a constraint.",
            "`dyn Summary` and `impl Summary` are the type-level forms."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "trait Summary {",
            "  fn summarize(&self) -> String; // `;` = required",
            "}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "impl Summary for Article {",
            "  fn summarize(&self) -> String {",
            "    self.headline.clone()",
            "  }",
            "}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Default Bodies"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Give the signature a body and implementors may omit it.",
            "`fn summarize(&self) -> String { String::from(\"(read more...)\") }`",
            "Implementors override it freely — no keyword needed.",
            "A default body may call other trait methods, even required ones."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "fn summarize(&self) -> String {",
            "  format!(\"(from {})\", self.author())",
            "}  // `author` has no default at all"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Receiver Forms"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Means"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`&self`",
              "borrow"
            ],
            [
              "`&mut self`",
              "mutate"
            ],
            [
              "`self`",
              "consume"
            ],
            [
              "none",
              "associated fn"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No receiver: `fn new() -> Self` — called as `Type::new()`.",
            "`Self` (capital) = the implementing type.",
            "`self` (lowercase) = the receiver value."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Trait Must Be In Scope",
          "text": "Methods are invisible until imported. `use std::io::Write;` before `.write_all()` is not decoration — it is required."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Marker Traits",
          "items": [
            "Zero methods, tag a property only.",
            "`Copy`, `Send`, `Sync`, `Sized`.",
            "Nothing to call — bounds read them."
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "impl Iterator for Counter {",
            "  type Item = u32;  // fixed forever",
            "  fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> { None }",
            "}"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Supertraits, Coherence & Newtypes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Supertraits & Constants"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "trait Summary: Display {}  // needs Display too",
            "trait Shape { const SIDES: u32; }",
            "// per-impl value, resolved at compile time"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Inherent method and trait method share a name — which runs?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "`impl Type { .. }` is an inherent impl; it wins on collision.",
            "Reach the trait one explicitly to disambiguate.",
            "No field inheritance, no constructors, no upcast hierarchy."
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "Trait::method(&x)",
            "<X as Trait>::method(&x)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Coherence",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "For any (trait, type) pair: at most one impl in the whole program.",
            "Otherwise a method call would be ambiguous across crates.",
            "Orphan rule enforces it: trait local, or type local."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Impl",
            "Local?",
            "Verdict"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`Display for MyType`",
              "type",
              "legal"
            ],
            [
              "`MyTrait for Vec<i32>`",
              "trait",
              "legal"
            ],
            [
              "`Display for Vec<i32>`",
              "neither",
              "illegal"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "E0117",
          "text": "`only traits defined in the current crate can be implemented for types defined outside of the crate`"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Why It Exists"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Two dependencies both write `impl Display for Vec<i32>`.",
            "Link them together — the call is unresolvable.",
            "Without the rule, any new impl is a breaking-change risk."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Newtype Workaround"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "struct Wrapper(Vec<String>);",
            "impl Display for Wrapper { /* legal: local */ }",
            "// reach the inner value with self.0"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Deref, Overlap & Sealed Traits",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Cost: `Wrapper` does not get `Vec`'s methods.",
            "Forward them with `impl Deref { type Target = Vec<String>; }`.",
            "Zero-cost at runtime: 1-field tuple struct has identical layout."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "impl Deref for Wrapper {",
            "  type Target = Vec<String>;",
            "  fn deref(&self) -> &Vec<String> {",
            "    &self.0  // w.len() now works",
            "  }",
            "}"
          ],
          "note": "forwards reads, not trait impls"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Fine Print",
          "items": [
            "`&T`, `&mut T`, `Box<T>` are fundamental — transparent to the rule.",
            "`impl MyTrait for &Foo` is checked against `Foo`.",
            "Covering rule: local type before any uncovered param."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Generic impl",
            "Allowed"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`impl<T> Foreign<T> for MyType`",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "`impl<T> Foreign<MyType> for T`",
              "no"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Overlap"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Rejected even when impls look disjoint to a human.",
            "No specialization on stable, so `impl<T> Foo for T` and `impl Foo for u8` clash.",
            "`error[E0119]: conflicting implementations of trait`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "impl<T> Foo for T {}",
            "impl Foo for u8 {}",
            "// E0119: u8 already covered by T"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Sealed Traits"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "mod sealed { pub trait S {} }",
            "pub trait Api: sealed::S {}",
            "impl sealed::S for MyType {}",
            "// downstream cannot name S"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Private supertrait = only your crate can implement `Api`.",
            "Callers still use `Api` methods freely.",
            "You may add methods later without a breaking change."
          ],
          "style": "check"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Error",
            "Cause",
            "Fix"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "E0117",
              "foreign + foreign",
              "newtype"
            ],
            [
              "E0119",
              "overlapping impls",
              "narrow bound"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Trait Bounds & impl Trait",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Blanket = API Commitment",
          "text": "A blanket impl in your crate permanently blocks users from writing their own impl for every type it covers."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Traits themselves can be private: `trait Sealed`.",
            "Basis of the sealed-trait pattern.",
            "Downstream crates then cannot implement your trait at all."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Unbounded Is Useless",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`fn largest<T>(list: &[T]) -> &T` cannot compare anything.",
            "`T` has no methods until a bound grants them.",
            "Add `T: PartialOrd` and `>` becomes legal."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "fn largest<T: PartialOrd>(",
            "  l: &[T]) -> &T {",
            "  let mut m = &l[0];",
            "  for x in l { if x > m { m = x } }",
            "  m",
            "}"
          ],
          "note": "`>` only legal with the bound"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "fn notify<T: Summary>(item: &T) {}",
            "fn notify2(item: &impl Summary) {} // sugar",
            "// no turbofish on the sugar form"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "`<T: Summary>`",
                "lines": [
                  "named param",
                  "turbofishable",
                  "`(a: &T, b: &T)` same type"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "`&impl Summary`",
                "lines": [
                  "anonymous param",
                  "no turbofish",
                  "two args may differ"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Stacking Bounds"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "fn notify<T: Summary + Display>(i: &T) {}",
            "fn f<T, U>(t: &T, u: &U) -> i32",
            "where T: Display + Clone, U: Clone + Debug",
            "{ 0 }"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`where` moves noise off the signature line.",
            "Strictly more expressive — bounds arbitrary types.",
            "`where Vec<T>: Debug` or `where Self: Sized` have no inline form."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Conditional Methods"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "impl<T: Display + PartialOrd> Pair<T> {",
            "  fn cmp_display(&self) {} // only for such T",
            "}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Return Position"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "fn make_adder(x: i32) -> impl Fn(i32) -> i32 {",
            "  move |y| x + y   // closure has no name",
            "}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Names one opaque concrete type — essential for closures and iterators.",
            "Returning an `Article` on one branch and a `Tweet` on another fails.",
            "Heterogeneous returns need `Box<dyn Summary>`."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Bound Placement & ?Sized",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "fn pick(b: bool)",
            "  -> Box<dyn Summary> {",
            "  if b { Box::new(Article {}) }",
            "  else { Box::new(Tweet {}) }",
            "}"
          ],
          "note": "or an enum, no allocation"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Where Bounds Go",
          "items": [
            "On the param: `<T: Clone>`.",
            "In a `where` clause: long or non-param bounds.",
            "On the impl block: gates every method inside."
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "The Hidden `Sized`",
          "items": [
            "Every `T` silently means `T: Sized`.",
            "Write `T: ?Sized` to opt out.",
            "Needed to accept `str` or `dyn Trait` behind a reference."
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "fn print<T: Display + ?Sized>(",
            "  x: &T) { println!(\"{x}\"); }",
            "print(\"hi\");  // T = str, unsized"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Signature",
            "Takes `&str`?"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`<T: Display>(x: &T)`",
              "no"
            ],
            [
              "`<T: Display + ?Sized>`",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "`(x: &dyn Display)`",
              "yes"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Only Behind A Pointer",
          "text": "A `?Sized` T is usable only via `&T`, `Box<T>` or `Rc<T>` — never by value, since its size is unknown."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Static Dispatch & Monomorphization",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Turbofish when inference stalls: `\"42\".parse::<i32>()`.",
            "Bounds are checked at the definition site, not the call site.",
            "The body must be valid for every type meeting the bounds."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Why does the error point at my generic body?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "The body is checked once, against the bounds only.",
            "`T` has a method only if a bound grants it.",
            "Fix: add the bound (`T: Clone`), not a cast."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "vs C++ Templates",
          "text": "Templates instantiate first and error deep inside the template. Rust states the contract up front, so callers get errors at their own call site."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Resolved At Compile Time",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Compiler knows the concrete type, emits a direct call to an address.",
            "Monomorphization stamps out one specialized copy per concrete type.",
            "`largest::<i32>` and `largest::<char>` become separate functions."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "let a = largest(&[1, 2, 3]);   // ::<i32>",
            "let b = largest(&['a', 'b']);  // ::<char>",
            "// two independent machine-code copies"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Zero-Cost, Literally",
          "text": "`fn add<T: Add>(a: T, b: T)` called with `i32` compiles to the same instruction as hand-written `i32` addition."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "fn add<T: Add<Output = T>>(",
            "  a: T, b: T) -> T { a + b }",
            "add(2_i32, 3); // one `add` instr"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Real Win Is Inlining"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Callee known, so LLVM inlines it.",
            "Then constant-folds, vectorizes, or deletes it entirely.",
            "Bigger win than the saved indirect jump."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "generic body",
              "monomorphize",
              "inline",
              "fold + vectorize",
              "one tight loop"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Iterator chains depend on this entirely.",
            "`v.iter().filter(..).map(..).sum()` becomes one loop.",
            "No closure calls survive to run time."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Hot loops and numeric kernels.",
            "Iterator adapters and closures.",
            "Library internals with a small type set."
          ],
          "style": "check"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Thin-Wrapper Outlining"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "fn open<P: AsRef<Path>>(p: P) {",
            "  open_inner(p.as_ref())  // shim duplicates",
            "}",
            "fn open_inner(p: &Path) {} // body does not"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The Cost of Monomorphization",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What It Costs"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Cost",
            "Cause",
            "Symptom"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "bloat",
              "N types x M fns",
              "i-cache pressure"
            ],
            [
              "compile time",
              "per codegen unit",
              "serde, diesel"
            ],
            [
              "no dylib",
              "body needed",
              "cannot cross C ABI"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Trait Objects & Fat Pointers",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "That is exactly how `std::fs::File::open` is written.",
            "Generic bodies must live in crate metadata, not object code.",
            "So generics cannot cross a C ABI or dynamic-library boundary."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Debug Toolkit",
          "items": [
            "`cargo llvm-lines` ranks fns by generated IR lines.",
            "Shows which generics bloat the binary.",
            "Run it before hand-outlining anything."
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Rules Of Thumb",
          "text": "Static for hot paths and small type sets. Watch for bloat when one generic is instantiated with dozens of types. Compile-time resolution is what lets associated types, `Self` returns, and generic methods work here — and not under `dyn`."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`impl Trait` args and returns are static dispatch too.",
            "The sugar changes syntax, never the dispatch strategy."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "`dyn Trait`",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Always behind a pointer: `&dyn Draw`, `Box<dyn Draw>`, `Rc<dyn Error>`.",
            "Bare `dyn Trait` is unsized — never a plain value.",
            "The type is erased; only the vtable remembers it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "let ui: Vec<Box<dyn Draw>> = vec![",
            "  Box::new(Button {}),",
            "  Box::new(Checkbox {}),  // mixed types",
            "];"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "The Motivating Case",
          "text": "A `Vec<T>` with generic `T` holds exactly one type. `Vec<Box<dyn Draw>>` holds buttons, checkboxes and text fields together."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Fat Pointer Layout"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "array",
          "spec": {
            "cells": [
              "data ptr",
              "vtable ptr"
            ],
            "indices": true,
            "pointers": [],
            "highlight": [],
            "dim": [],
            "caption": "&dyn Draw = 2 words"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "size_of::<&dyn Draw>() == 2 * size_of::<&u8>()",
            "// 16 bytes on a 64-bit target"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Vtable is static, one per (type, trait) pair.",
            "Holds destructor pointer, size, alignment.",
            "Then one function pointer per trait method."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "One Call, Three Steps"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "load vtable ptr",
              "load fixed slot",
              "indirect call",
              "`self` = data ptr"
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Dynamic Dispatch Costs & Coercion",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Coercion Is One-Way"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`&Button` becomes `&dyn Draw` implicitly — unsizing coercion.",
            "Lossless going in; getting back needs downcasting.",
            "`dyn Any` + `downcast_ref::<Button>()` gives `Option<&Button>`.",
            "Only stable route back, and it requires `T: 'static`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Cost",
            "Detail"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "no inlining",
              "kills const-prop"
            ],
            [
              "BTB mispredict",
              "varying receiver"
            ],
            [
              "absolute",
              "a few ns/call"
            ],
            [
              "binary",
              "smaller, faster build"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Matters in tight loops; invisible for I/O or per-frame work.",
            "One vtable per type beats one code copy per type.",
            "Standard answer for plugin boundaries and callback registries.",
            "Also stops generic params from infecting every signature."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "`Box<dyn Error>`",
          "text": "`fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>>` is the ergonomic error type: anything implementing `Error` converts in through `?`."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Composing `dyn`",
          "items": [
            "`dyn Trait + Send + 'static` is fine — auto traits and lifetimes add freely.",
            "Only one non-auto trait per object.",
            "No `dyn Read + Write`; use `trait ReadWrite: Read + Write`."
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Dyn Compatibility",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The rules a trait must satisfy before `dyn Trait` is a legal type.",
            "Renamed from \"object safety\" to \"dyn compatibility\".",
            "One test: can the vtable be built without knowing the concrete type?"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Dyn Compatibility Rules",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Rule",
            "Broken by",
            "Victim"
          ],
          "rows": [
